WEBVTT

00:00:04.500 --> 00:00:05.980
Picture an agent asked to look through

00:00:05.980 --> 00:00:07.930
the open issues on a public repo.

00:00:08.120 --> 00:00:09.900
One issue was written by a stranger,

00:00:09.900 --> 00:00:11.600
and buried in it is a paragraph

00:00:11.600 --> 00:00:13.180
addressed not to the maintainer,

00:00:13.180 --> 00:00:14.300
but to the agent.

00:00:14.300 --> 00:00:15.320
The agent reads it,

00:00:15.320 --> 00:00:17.150
and helpfully does what it says.

00:00:17.160 --> 00:00:19.270
Private data ends up in public.

00:00:19.440 --> 00:00:20.540
Nothing was broken.

00:00:20.540 --> 00:00:22.730
Every part worked as designed.

00:00:23.616 --> 00:00:26.186
Application security rests on one idea:

00:00:26.216 --> 00:00:28.426
data should never run as instructions.

00:00:28.736 --> 00:00:31.206
Language models don't have that separation.

00:00:31.456 --> 00:00:32.436
Your request,

00:00:32.436 --> 00:00:33.196
a file,

00:00:33.196 --> 00:00:34.176
a web page,

00:00:34.176 --> 00:00:35.116
a tool result.

00:00:35.116 --> 00:00:36.076
To the model,

00:00:36.076 --> 00:00:37.466
it's all just tokens.

00:00:37.776 --> 00:00:39.106
That's prompt injection.

00:00:39.156 --> 00:00:41.096
Someone puts text where your agent will

00:00:41.096 --> 00:00:41.496
read it,

00:00:41.496 --> 00:00:42.656
and the text says:

00:00:42.656 --> 00:00:43.746
do this instead.

00:00:44.685 --> 00:00:46.765
The model is not a security boundary.

00:00:46.765 --> 00:00:49.025
A defence that works nearly every time

00:00:49.025 --> 00:00:50.245
is a good spam filter,

00:00:50.245 --> 00:00:52.085
and a terrible lock.

00:00:52.085 --> 00:00:54.425
Prompt injection is unsolved.

00:00:54.425 --> 00:00:56.125
So plan as if it will happen,

00:00:56.125 --> 00:00:57.815
and ask a better question:

00:00:57.865 --> 00:00:59.475
when the agent is tricked,

00:00:59.525 --> 00:01:01.135
what's the worst it can do?

00:01:01.888 --> 00:01:03.548
The most useful model here is the

00:01:03.548 --> 00:01:04.918
lethal trifecta.

00:01:05.068 --> 00:01:06.658
Access to private data.

00:01:06.708 --> 00:01:08.668
Exposure to untrusted content.

00:01:08.668 --> 00:01:10.358
And a way to send data out.

00:01:10.388 --> 00:01:11.898
Any two are manageable.

00:01:12.088 --> 00:01:13.588
All three in one session,

00:01:13.588 --> 00:01:15.108
and anyone who can get text in

00:01:15.108 --> 00:01:16.548
front of your agent can get your

00:01:16.548 --> 00:01:17.268
data out.

00:01:17.268 --> 00:01:19.028
So remove at least one leg,

00:01:19.028 --> 00:01:20.238
for every session.

00:01:21.012 --> 00:01:23.092
Untrusted text gets in through more doors

00:01:23.092 --> 00:01:23.912
than you'd think.

00:01:23.912 --> 00:01:25.352
Issues and comments.

00:01:25.352 --> 00:01:26.282
Web pages.

00:01:26.392 --> 00:01:27.352
Log lines,

00:01:27.352 --> 00:01:29.522
where a username can carry a payload.

00:01:29.652 --> 00:01:32.202
Tool descriptions from other people's servers.

00:01:32.452 --> 00:01:34.332
Even the instruction files in a repo

00:01:34.332 --> 00:01:35.352
you just cloned.

00:01:35.352 --> 00:01:37.252
Pointing an agent at someone else's repo

00:01:37.252 --> 00:01:39.362
means running someone else's prompt.

00:01:40.094 --> 00:01:42.014
And data leaves through more than email.

00:01:42.014 --> 00:01:43.754
An image link with a secret in

00:01:43.754 --> 00:01:44.594
its address.

00:01:44.594 --> 00:01:45.784
A public comment.

00:01:45.814 --> 00:01:47.124
A web request.

00:01:47.174 --> 00:01:48.394
Even a name lookup.

00:01:48.394 --> 00:01:50.314
If the agent can write anywhere an

00:01:50.314 --> 00:01:51.494
outsider can read,

00:01:51.494 --> 00:01:52.524
that's an exit.

00:01:53.249 --> 00:01:53.909
Skills,

00:01:53.909 --> 00:01:55.909
plugins and servers are dependencies,

00:01:55.909 --> 00:01:56.589
too.

00:01:56.589 --> 00:01:57.389
Read them,

00:01:57.389 --> 00:01:57.849
pin them,

00:01:57.849 --> 00:01:59.049
and review changes,

00:01:59.049 --> 00:02:01.569
because a tool's description can quietly change

00:02:01.569 --> 00:02:02.999
after you approved it.

00:02:03.631 --> 00:02:05.181
The defenses come in layers.

00:02:05.271 --> 00:02:06.051
Scoped,

00:02:06.051 --> 00:02:07.301
short -lived tokens.

00:02:07.331 --> 00:02:09.991
A reader agent that sees untrusted text

00:02:09.991 --> 00:02:11.481
but holds no privileges.

00:02:11.751 --> 00:02:13.271
Passing only checked,

00:02:13.271 --> 00:02:15.591
structured data to an actor that does.

00:02:15.591 --> 00:02:16.901
A closed network.

00:02:16.911 --> 00:02:19.041
A human approving anything outbound.

00:02:19.171 --> 00:02:21.161
And a log the agent can't edit.

00:02:21.899 --> 00:02:23.099
Think of a harbour pilot.

00:02:23.099 --> 00:02:25.139
They take the helm through unfamiliar waters,

00:02:25.139 --> 00:02:27.079
but they don't get the keys to

00:02:27.079 --> 00:02:27.759
the hold.

00:02:27.759 --> 00:02:29.049
Let your agents steer.

00:02:29.059 --> 00:02:30.189
Keep the keys.

00:02:30.339 --> 00:02:31.369
Next up:

00:02:31.379 --> 00:02:32.689
articulating intent.
